httpd means to pass methods other than GET, HEAD and POST, such as PUT,
DELETE and OPTIONS, to CGI programs, and to answer them with 501 Not
Implemented for plain files. But it computed the length of the copied
method name as the difference between the return value of safe_strncpy
and the destination, and safe_strncpy returns the destination, so the
length was always 0 and every such request was answered with 400 Bad
Request, CGI or not.

Take the length with strlen instead. Also read Content-Length for every
method but GET and HEAD rather than for POST alone, so that the body of
a PUT reaches the CGI program on its standard input, with CONTENT_LENGTH
set, as a POST body does.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -2686,7 +2686,7 @@
 		goto found;
 	/* For CGI, allow DELETE, PUT, OPTIONS, etc too */
 	prequest = alloca(16);
-	un = safe_strncpy((char*)prequest, iobuf, 16) - prequest;
+	un = strlen(safe_strncpy((char*)prequest, iobuf, 16));
 	if (un < 1 || un >= 15)
 		send_headers_and_exit(HTTP_BAD_REQUEST);
 #endif
@@ -2848,8 +2848,10 @@
 #endif
 		dbg("header:'%s'\n", iobuf);
 #if ENABLE_FEATURE_HTTPD_CGI
-		/* Only POST needs to know POST_length */
-		if (prequest == request_POST && STRNCASECMP(iobuf, "Content-Length:") == 0) {
+		/* Only POST, PUT etc. need to know POST_length */
+		if (prequest != request_GET && prequest != request_HEAD
+		 && STRNCASECMP(iobuf, "Content-Length:") == 0
+		) {
 			tptr = skip_whitespace(iobuf + sizeof("Content-Length:") - 1);
 			if (!tptr[0])
 				send_headers_and_exit(HTTP_BAD_REQUEST);
